# S3 (plan 16) — THE CLIENT MESHES THE GROUND ITSELF, AND GETS THE SERVER'S ANSWER.
#
# S2 proved the client's VOXELS are the server's. This proves the step that matters:
# run the server's own mesher over the client's own cache and the triangles come out
# identical, checksum for checksum. That is what lets the 1 MB-per-chunk `M:` text
# frames go away — the receiver can derive what it was being sent.
#
# ⚠ THE COMPARISON IS GUARDED, AND THE GUARD IS THE FINDING. The mesher reads two
# cells PAST the tile it builds, so a tile whose margin has not arrived yet is not a
# disagreement — it is a question asked too early. `moros_terrain::tile_ready` answers
# it from the cache's own chunks, and `probe/s3/s3_oracle_boundary.loft` sweeps nine
# cache shapes to show the answer is exact rather than cautious: 580 READY tiles, 0
# wrong, and not one held tile that would have been fine.
#
# ⚠ CONTROL, SEEN RED: before the guard, this same run read `ground 41 bad 7` — and
# every one of the 7 was a tile the server had sent but the client had not yet
# received. The arithmetic was never wrong; the timing was.
rate 0
# ⚠ THE SNAPSHOT IS WHAT OPENS THE BROWSER, and the browser IS the client under
# test. Dropping it because this gate judges no picture left the run with nobody to
# compare anything — every verdict line simply never arrived.
at 0 0 0
verb raise
wait rebuilt
step 20
snap s3-mesh
step 20
wait cache
wait ground
step 20
# ⚠ THE LAST WORD, NOT THE FIRST. `wait` returns the earliest matching status, and
# these tallies are cumulative — the first `ground` report is always the instant
# before any evidence exists.
last cache
# ⚠ S4 NEEDS A REBUILD AFTER THE OPT-IN, or it measures nothing. The client only earns
# the right to draw its own ground after four agreements, and by then the opening stream
# is long finished — so with no further edit the counters never move again and the last
# report is the one from BEFORE the opt-in, reading `held 0`. That is not the deletion
# failing, it is the gate looking at the wrong instant.
#
# So: raise the ground again, now, with the client deriving. Every ground mesh rebuilt
# from here is one the server builds and does NOT put on the wire for that client.
verb raise
wait rebuilt
step 20
verb raise
step 20
# `derive N of M ground sent S held H` — what the server PUT ON THE WIRE against what it
# withheld. Reported when the counters change, never at the opt-in.
last ground
